ir schools in protest</a><span> of crumbling facilities and a shortage of t=
eachers.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#3=
33;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Then, on Tuesday, teachers and other public worke=
rs joined them in an expression of solidarity, and as part of their own ral=
ly against proposed wage freezes.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25=
px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>And before the students =
and the teachers, there were the fishermen. Last month, they blocked ports =
and fuel terminals to protest the cost of diesel.</span></p><p style=3D"fon=
t:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The caus=
es of the chaos may appear disparate. But they have a common root: France=
=E2=80=99s two-decade-long struggle to fund its state =E2=80=94 a struggle =
now being exacerbated by high borrowing costs, surging fuel prices and poli=
tical uncertainty.</span></p><table border=3D"0" cellpadding=3D"0" cellspac=
