e next European country to tumble into a full-blown financial crisis.</span=
></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 =
15px"><span>The country=E2=80=99s debt stands at roughly $4 trillion, more =
than its entire economy. The annual cost of servicing that debt is projecte=
d to increase to roughly $100 billion in 2027. The street clashes have not =
helped traders=E2=80=99 jitters.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25p=
x georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>These deepening anxieties=
 do not mean France is destined to suffer a Greece-style debt crisis or the=
 kind of market backlash that toppled a British prime minister, Liz Truss, =
after she proposed budget-busting tax cuts in 2022.</span></p><p style=3D"f=
ont:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Econom=
ists point out that other countries, including the United States, face pres=
sure on their public finances because of rising interest rates. France=E2=
=80=99s anchor role in the European Union, they said, means that the bloc=
=E2=80=99s other members would bail it out, should it ever come to that.</s=
pan></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0=
 0 15px"><span>=E2=80=9CFrance=E2=80=99s position looks unsustainable and t=
he bond markets are saying that, but they=E2=80=99re not screaming it yet,=
=E2=80=9D said Kenneth Rogoff, a professor of economics at Harvard and a sc=
holar of financial crises. =E2=80=9CIt=E2=80=99s a slow-motion train wreck.=
=E2=80=9D</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#=
333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>But France may be heading toward a reckoning: Th=
e country can no longer afford the generous welfare state that has always b=
een so central to its identity.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px=
 georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>=E2=80=9CThat is the deepe=
